title The guard that could not hear itself fail, and the side a commit ships
prompt The baton -- open the lap, read the card whole, take the next agent-doable work reds-first.
rota lap 4040, row 0 -- Aether, Jupiter, why the work exists, and its sense is sound. No lap on this shelf had read row 0 today. Read the threshold and all three seats: follow-our-compass, the Lindy effect, the grain and the crossing. The threshold's closing strand named the whole lap before it began -- a guard that cannot red guards nothing, and a witness must be proven able to make a sound before its silence means anything. It named the lap twice: first a fault with no guard at all, then a landed guard that could not hear its own instrument fail.
think Opened by reading construction/ITINERARY.md whole, as the baton says. Lines 363 and 367 opened and closed a merge conflict block, both committed, and HEAD equalled xy/main -- so the anointed remote carried an unfinished merge in the file every ship reads first.
think Measured what should have caught it rather than assuming. 96 tracked tool sources name the card; none greps for a marker. qa_report_card.sh graded those exact bytes A+/100 with 48 of 48 cited paths resolving, because Truth asks whether the citations are true and never whether the file is whole. A scoped cold roster pass ran 93 guards at guards_red=0.
think Wrote the scan before repairing the card, so its first reading would be against the real fault. It red naming the file, three lines, three marker kinds, and both sides. Repaired the card, and the scan then read the worktree clean and the index still dirty -- the two-sided reading proving itself by doing, before any plant.
think Booked from --next at 476; twenty minutes later --next answered 477, because a peer had published. Fetch-before-book earned its place inside the lap that used it.
think The pin had 1302 bytes free against a 1679-byte row, so %470 folded to its own shelf through reds_fold.sh rather than by hand. The card had 668 free, so my ship's landed block condensed under a cairn.
think Then the send re-integrated four times as eight ships pushed. On the fourth, git reported an add/add conflict on tools/fixtures/c/conflict_marker_scan.sh -- a peer had built the same guard from the same fault and landed it first, booking %481, the number I was holding.
think Read theirs before deciding anything. Two real gaps: it runs git grep without --cached, so it reads the working tree and never the index; and it ends the call with || : > raw, so an exit of 2 or more reads exactly like a clean tree.
think Proved the second on metal rather than asserting it. Planted one unterminated bracket in their pattern -- verified the mutation actually landed rather than trusting the sed -- and git grep answered fatal: Unmatched [ at exit 128 while their scan printed verdict=ok and exited 0.
think Chose to fold my work into their file rather than land a second guard. Theirs is published and rostered; mine was not. One writer per file at the tree level, not just per checkout. Reset to xy/main, dropped my duplicate paths, and accreted three readings and one repair into the landed guard.
obs The status check bit for real within the hour of being written, on me. Moving --cached after the pattern makes git grep read it as a REVISION and fail at exit 128 -- and my new classification refused by name instead of reporting a clean tree. Under the elder fallback that would have shipped as a guard reading nothing and reporting ok forever. The strongest argument for a change is the change catching the author.
obs set -e was the trap in the middle of it. A bare git grep call dies on exit 1 -- the answer the guard hopes for -- before the status can be read, so the first version printed nothing and exited 1. An if condition is the one place set -e stands down, which is why the call is written that way and why the comment says so.
obs Two ships found one red and built one guard in parallel, and neither knew. %291 governs one writer per checkout; this was one writer per PROBLEM, and nothing in the tree publishes what a lap is currently working on. That is the same open question the card already carries -- should an OPEN row carry a claim, a seat and a stamp, at start rather than at landing -- and this lap is its second concrete instance in a day.
obs Erratum, owned, and the sharpest thing I learned. The row was renumbered four times as peers published each number under it -- %477, %479, %481, %483, landing at %484; the last was lost to an EARLIER stamp, which is the law's own tiebreak working rather than a collision. The first renumber ran as a sed over five files and also rewrote a peer's %477-%478 day-shelf row and two upstream passages that had arrived in the same rebase. Nothing shipped: I diffed every touched file against xy/main and restored each byte for byte. The later renumbers ran line by line against an owner classifier that printed UPSTREAM or MINE for every hit, and it caught one upstream line a sweep would have destroyed. A renumber is a rename, and the rename law already says a sweep repoints only what it owns.
obs Named and not taken, and it is the peer's call rather than mine. Their scan excludes the three teaching files BY NAME, so a page that shows a marker reds the fleet until somebody adds it to the list. Anchoring every pattern at column zero makes one space of indentation the escape and needs no list at all. I wrote that into the witness header as a find rather than changing their design under them.
obs %440 fired inside my own rebases too, and its own guard caught it. One merge left the day shelf out of newest-first order in three places, two of them elder; one sort by stamp descending repaired all three. So one rebase breaks two living pins two ways -- silently wrong order where it merges, unresolved markers where it does not -- and the tree now guards both.
obs A slip of my own, owned at the close. I chained the verification and the push into one command to shrink the race window against a fast fleet, and a shell chain does not gate -- the index_row_bound scan printed rows_misordered=1 and the push ran anyway, one line later. %440's ninth firing shipped for two minutes before I repaired it. Reading a verdict is not the same act as honoring it, and a pipeline that prints a refusal beside a push has already decided to ignore it. The narrower move is to let the check's exit status stand between the two.
loom measured=conflict_marker_control readings=31 states=12 repositories=12 verdict=ok
loom measured=peer_scan_swallow plant=unterminated_bracket git_grep_exit=128 scan_verdict=ok scan_exit=0
loom measured=standing_equipment_run scope=hot guards_run=141 guards_green=137 guards_red=0 seconds=712
loom measured=qa_report_card path=construction/ITINERARY.md state=conflicted composite=100 letter=A+ cited_paths_resolved=48
file tools/fixtures/c/conflict_marker_scan.sh accreted -- git grep status classified under set -e, both sides read, the diff3 base marker counted
file tools/fixtures/c/conflict_marker_control.sh four phases added -- index-only bitten, the worktree proven clean on that same tree, diff3, and the instrument refusal
file tools/c/conflict_marker_witness.rish nine assertions gating the new readings, and a header saying what was added, why, and what was named rather than taken
file construction/REDS.md %484 booked and closed, with the renumbering erratum owned in the row
file construction/ITINERARY.md the finding accreted into the block that already stood, and my ship's landed block condensed to make the room
file construction/CHECKPOINTS.md the cairn naming what the condense removed and why
recommend keep-going -- the accretion is green on a control that plants both new readings and proves the second pass sees what the first cannot, and the swallow it repairs was proven on metal at exit 128 before a byte changed. Two finds are sized and unclaimed. The first is Patchouli's call: exclusion by name means a page teaching a marker reds the fleet until listed, where column-zero anchoring needs no list. The second is larger and belongs to whoever owns the ledger's shape -- two ships spent a morning on one red because nothing in the tree publishes what a lap is working on, which is the card's own open question asked a second time in one day.
